\chapter{Configuration Space}
\label{ch:configuration-space}

The configuration space is the unifying abstraction of robot motion:
once it is in place, planning, control, and localization all become
statements about the same underlying object. This chapter introduces
the concept, its structure, and its role in motion planning.

\section{Motivation: Why Configuration Space?}
\label{sec:why-cspace}

The central problem of mobile robotics is the following: given a robot
in an environment with obstacles, move it from an initial configuration
to a final one without collisions. Stated directly in the workspace,
this problem is hard: the robot has an extent, the obstacles have
arbitrary shapes, and checking whether a given placement is collision-free
requires geometric reasoning.

The \emph{configuration space} (C-space) makes the problem tractable. 
The idea is to replace the motion of an extended body among obstacles with the motion of a
\emph{point} in an abstract space, where obstacles have been ``inflated''
to account for the robot's shape. 
A robot is a continuous body, i.e.\ an infinite set of points.
Describing it point by point is impossible. The key idea is to
represent it with a \emph{finite} set of parameters: the
configuration.
The solution is to define a suitable space (the C-space) in which
\emph{the robot is represented as a point}, and the obstacles are
represented as regions of that space that the point cannot enter.
This reduction turns a complex geometric problem into a path-search problem in a topological space,
for which a large body of algorithms exists
\cite{choset2005principles, siciliano2025foundations}.

\section{Modeling a Robot}
\label{sec:modeling-robot}

A robot is a continuous body: an infinite set of points. Describing it
point by point is impossible. The key idea is to find a \emph{finite}
set of parameters that completely determines the position of every
point of the robot. We model a robot as a collection of \( n_b \) rigid
bodies moving in a workspace \( W \). The workspace is the set of
physical points in which the robot can move. 

\subsection{Configuration and Degrees of Freedom}
\label{sec:configuration-dof}

\begin{definition}[Configuration]
A \emph{configuration} \( q \) is a complete specification of the
position of every point of the robot. It is represented as a vector
\( q = [q_1, \dots, q_n]^\top \), where \( n \) is the number of
\emph{degrees of freedom} (DOF) of the robot.
\end{definition}

The configuration vector $q = (q_1, \dots, q_n)$, where each $q_i$ is a
generalized coordinate (either Cartesian or angular). The Cartesian
coordinates identify the position of points of the robot (e.g.\ the
center of mass of a rigid body), and take values in $\mathbb{R}$. The
angular coordinates identify the orientation of the rigid bodies, and
take values in $SO(2)$ or $SO(3)$.

Thus, a \emph{configuration} is a minimal set of parameters that identifies the posture of the robot
in \( W \): if the parameters are known, the position of every point of
the robot is known.

\begin{nb}[Special Orthogonal Group SO(n)]
The Special orthogonal group SO(n) refers to the group of rotations in three-dimensional 
space that preserve the orientation and the Euclidean structure. 
It is characterized by orthogonal matrices with determinant equal to one, 
representing transformations that maintain distances and angles.

All the matrices R in SO(n) satisfy the following properties:
\begin{itemize}
    \item $R^\top R = I$, where $R^\top$ is the transpose of $R$ and
          $I$ is the identity matrix;
    \item $\det(R) = 1$, ensuring that the transformation preserves
          orientation;
    \item The set of all such matrices forms a group under matrix
          multiplication.
\end{itemize}
\end{nb}


\section{The Configuration Space and the Workspace}
\label{sec:cspace-definition}

\begin{definition}[Configuration Space]
The \emph{configuration space} \( C \) is the set of all possible
configurations of the robot. Formally, \( C \subseteq \mathbb{R}^n \),
although in many cases it carries a richer structure: for instance,
\( C = \mathbb{R}^2 \times \mathcal{S}^1 \) for a planar robot with
orientation, where \( \mathcal{S}^1 \) denotes the unit circle.
\end{definition}

For a collection of \( n_b \) rigid bodies moving in the plane,
\( \dim(C) = 3 n_b \) (two translations and one rotation per body).
In three dimensions, \( \dim(C) = 6 n_b \) (three translations and
three rotations). For a manipulator with \( n \) joints,
\( \dim(C) = n \). In all cases,
\[
\dim(C) = \text{DOF of the robot},
\]
which in general differs from \( \dim(W) \).

When such a structure of $C$ is represented (\( C = \mathbb{R}^i \times \mathcal{S}^j \)),
we are talking about the \emph{Special Euclidean Group}.
\begin{nb}[Special Euclidean Group SE(n)]
The Special Euclidean group $\mathbf{SE}(n)$ describes the orientation-preserving isometries of the Euclidean space $\mathbb{R}^n$, where $n \in \mathbb{N}$ is an integer. It represents rigid transformations (combinations of rotations and translations) that preserve both distances between points and the orientation of the space.

This group can be represented using homogeneous transformation matrices as:
\[
\mathbf{SE}(n) = \left\{ X = \begin{pmatrix} R & p \\ 0_{1 \times n} & 1 \end{pmatrix} \in \mathbb{R}^{(n+1) \times (n+1)} \;\middle|\; R^\top R = I_n, \; \det(R) = 1, \; p \in \mathbb{R}^n \right\}
\]

The matrix components are defined as follows:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Rotation matrix ($R$):} An element of the Special Orthogonal group $\mathbf{SO}(n)$, characterized by $R^\top R = I_n$ and $\det(R) = 1$, which handles changes in orientation without deformation or reflection.
    \item \textbf{Translation vector ($p$):} A vector $p \in \mathbb{R}^n$ representing the rigid displacement in space.
    \item \textbf{Bottom row ($0_{1 \times n}$ and $1$):} A padding row that establishes homogeneous coordinates, allowing the composition of multiple rigid transformations through simple matrix multiplication.
\end{itemize}
      
\end{nb}

By definition, the dimension of the configuration space $C$ always equals the robot's degrees of freedom 
($\dim(C) = \text{DOF}$), representing the minimum number of independent 
parameters required to uniquely specify its state. 
\emph{C-space} is the topological variety of all the 
possible configurations the robot can assume.
In general, this does not coincide with the dimension of 
the workspace $W$ ($\dim(W) \neq \dim(C)$), which depends on the physical task space 
(e.g., position in $\mathbb{R}^3$ or full pose in $SE(3)$)
rather than the robot's kinematic structure.

\subsection{Workspace vs Configuration Space}
\label{sec:workspace-vs-cspace}

To recap:

\begin{itemize}
    \item The \textbf{workspace} (${W}$) is the physical space
          in which the robot moves (e.g.\ \( \mathbb{R}^2 \) or
          \( \mathbb{R}^3 \)), expressed in world coordinates.
    \item The \textbf{configuration space} \( C \) is the abstract space
          of all configurations the robot can assume.
\end{itemize}

A planar robot that can both translate and rotate has a 3-dimensional
C-space, even though it lives in a 2-dimensional workspace. A free
rigid body in \( \mathbb{R}^3 \) has a 6-dimensional C-space, while
its workspace is 3-dimensional.

\section{Examples}
\label{sec:cspace-examples}

\subsection{Point Robot}
\label{subsec:point-robot}

If the robot is a point translating in \( \mathbb{R}^2 \), then
\( q = (x, y) \) and \( C = \mathbb{R}^2 \). The C-space coincides
with the workspace, and the C-obstacles are the original obstacles.

\subsection{Disk Robot}
\label{subsec:disk-robot}

A disk-shaped robot of radius \( r \) translating in \( \mathbb{R}^2 \)
has \( q = (x, y) \), where \( (x, y) \) is the center of the disk.
Again \( C = \mathbb{R}^2 \), but each obstacle is \emph{inflated} by
the robot's radius: a point obstacle becomes a disk of radius \( r \),
and a polygonal obstacle is expanded by the \emph{Minkowski sum} with
the robot's disk. After this inflation, the disk robot can be treated
as a point in \( C \).

\subsection{Robot with Orientation: \( \mathbb{R}^2 \times \mathcal{S}^1 \)}
\label{subsec:cspace-orientation}

If the robot is a disk that can also rotate, then
\( q = (x, y, \theta) \) and \( C = \mathbb{R}^2 \times \mathcal{S}^1 \),
where \( \mathcal{S}^1 \) is the unit circle (the angle \( \theta \) is
periodic). This space has a non-trivial topology: it is \emph{not}
\( \mathbb{R}^3 \), because \( \theta \) and \( \theta + 2\pi \) denote
the same configuration. The intuitive picture is a \emph{cylinder}:
one linear dimension \( (x, y) \) and one circular dimension
\( (\theta) \).

\subsection{Planar 2-Link Manipulator: \( \mathcal{T}^2 \)}
\label{subsec:cspace-2link}

For a planar manipulator with two revolute joints,
\( q = (\theta_1, \theta_2) \) and
\( C = \mathcal{S}^1 \times \mathcal{S}^1 = \mathcal{T}^2 \), the
\emph{2-dimensional torus}. Each joint is a circle, and the product
of two circles is a torus. This is a classic example of how the
topology of \( C \) can differ radically from that of
\( \mathbb{R}^n \).

\subsection{Rigid Body in 3D: \( SE(3) \)}
\label{subsec:cspace-se3}

A free rigid body in \( \mathbb{R}^3 \) has 6 DOF: three translational
and three rotational. Its C-space is
\( C = \mathbb{R}^3 \times SO(3) = SE(3) \), the \emph{special
Euclidean group} of dimension 3. The rotational part \( SO(3) \) is
not \( \mathbb{R}^3 \): it is a compact 3-dimensional manifold.


