\section{Topology of Configuration Space}
\label{sec:cspace-topology}

The configuration space \( C \) of a robot is not always a
Euclidean space. Its \emph{topology} --- the way it is glued together
globally --- can differ substantially from that of \( \mathbb{R}^n \),
and this has direct consequences on the design of planning and control
algorithms. The canonical example in this course is the planar 2R
manipulator, which we use to answer three questions in order: what is
\( C \) for this robot, why does the naive picture of it as a
plane fail, and what is the right space to work in.

\subsection{The 2R Planar Manipulator: \( C = SO(2)\times SO(2) \)}
\label{subsec:cspace-2R-topology}

Consider a planar manipulator with two revolute joints. Its
configuration is \( q = (q_1, q_2)^T \), where \( q_1 \) is the angle of
the first link with respect to the base frame and \( q_2 \) the angle of
the second link with respect to the first (\cref{fig:cspace-arm-equivalence},
left). Each joint angle is a planar rotation, i.e.\ an element of
\( SO(2) \cong \mathcal{S}^1 \), the circle. The configuration space is
therefore the product of two circles,
\[
C = SO(2) \times SO(2) \cong \mathcal{S}^1 \times \mathcal{S}^1
= \mathcal{T}^2,
\]
the \emph{2-dimensional torus}.

The most immediate way to draw \( C \) is to plot every
configuration as a point of the plane \( (q_1, q_2) \in \mathbb{R}^2 \)
(\cref{fig:cspace-arm-equivalence}, right): each point is a posture of the
arm. This picture is, however, misleading.

