\subsection{The Remaining Problem: Closeness Is Not Preserved}
\label{subsec:cspace-closeness}

Injectivity is not enough. A good representation of \( C \)
must also respect its \emph{topology}: postures that are close must be
represented by points that are close, and vice versa. The square fails
this test. Take a small \( \varepsilon > 0 \) and consider
\( q_\varepsilon = (\varepsilon, q_2) \) and
\( q_{2\pi-\varepsilon} = (2\pi - \varepsilon, q_2) \)
(\cref{fig:cspace-torus-square}). In the square they are almost as far
apart as possible, at distance \( 2\pi - 2\varepsilon \); but the two
arms differ only by a rotation of \( 2\varepsilon \) of the first joint,
and as \( \varepsilon \to 0 \) they coincide (\cref{fig:cspace-torus-square},
right). Equivalently, a continuous motion of the arm across
\( q_1 = 0 \) (or \( q_2 = 0 \)) appears in the square as a jump from one
edge to the opposite one.

In mathematical terms, \( \Phi \) restricted to the half-open square is a
continuous bijection onto \( \mathcal{T}^2 \) whose inverse is
discontinuous along the edges, so it is not a homeomorphism: the fundamental
domain describes the same set of postures, but with the wrong topology.

\subsection{Gluing the Square: Why \( C \) Is a Torus}
\label{subsec:cspace-gluing}

The fix is to put back what the fundamental domain removed, namely the
identifications \( q_i = 0 \equiv 2\pi \) on the boundary, so that points
on opposite edges become neighbours again. This can be done in two steps
(\cref{fig:cspace-torus-3d}). Gluing the vertical edges
\( q_1 = 0 \equiv 2\pi \) turns the square into a cylinder; gluing the
two circular boundaries \( q_2 = 0 \equiv 2\pi \) of the cylinder closes it
into a torus. The points \( q_\varepsilon \) and \( q_{2\pi-\varepsilon} \)
now lie on the two sides of the seam and are indeed close to each other:
the torus respects the topology of \( C \).

That the result is always a torus is not a coincidence but a consequence of
the product structure: each joint contributes a circle, and the product of
two circles is \( \mathcal{T}^2 \) (for \( n \) revolute joints,
\( \mathcal{T}^n \)). The torus is also a \emph{manifold}: around every
point, seam included, a small neighbourhood looks like a disc of
\( \mathbb{R}^2 \). Angular coordinates \( (q_1, q_2) \) are thus perfectly
good \emph{locally}, and the only obstruction is to use them
\emph{globally} with a single chart, which is what the square attempts to
do. This is typical: configuration spaces are usually manifolds described
by angular coordinates.

