        \def\rx{1.0}\def\ry{0.32}
        \draw[thick] (-\rx,0) -- (-\rx,\S);
        \draw[thick] (\rx,0)  -- (\rx,\S);
        % bottom circle (front solid, back dashed)
        \draw[thick, blue!70!black] (-\rx,0) arc[start angle=180,end angle=360,x radius=\rx,y radius=\ry];
        \draw[thick, blue!70!black, dashed] (-\rx,0) arc[start angle=180,end angle=0,x radius=\rx,y radius=\ry];
        % top circle
        \draw[thick, blue!70!black] (0,\S) ellipse ({\rx} and {\ry});
        % seam (front)
        \draw[very thick, red!80!black] (0,-\ry) -- (0,\S-\ry);
        \draw[->, very thick, red!80!black] (0,0.9-\ry) -- (0,2.1-\ry);
        \draw[->, thick, blue!70!black] (-0.45,\S-\ry-0.02) -- (0.45,\S-\ry-0.02);
        \draw[->, thick, blue!70!black] (-0.45,-\ry-0.02) -- (0.45,-\ry-0.02);
        \node[note] at (0,-0.95) {(b) cylinder};
      \end{scope}

      % ================= glue 2
      \draw[->, very thick, gray] (7.9,1.5) -- (8.9,1.5);
      \node[blue!70!black, font=\scriptsize, align=center] at (8.4,2.05) {glue\\$q_2$};

      % ================= (c) torus
      \begin{scope}[shift={(11.5,1.55)}, scale=0.82]
        \pgfmathsetmacro{\TR}{2.0}
        \pgfmathsetmacro{\Tr}{0.8}
        \pgfmathsetmacro{\Ts}{0.6}
        \pgfmathsetmacro{\Tc}{0.8}
        \def\ug{-60}   % position of the seam q_1 = 0 = 2pi
        % outline and hole
        \draw[thick] (0,0) ellipse ({\TR+\Tr} and {\Ts*\TR+\Tr});
        \draw[thick] (0,0) ellipse ({\TR-\Tr} and {\Ts*\TR-\Tr});
        % blue: q_2 = 0 = 2pi  (outer equator)
        \draw[very thick, blue!70!black, samples=60, domain=180:360, variable=\t]
             plot ({(\TR+\Tr)*cos(\t)}, {\Ts*(\TR+\Tr)*sin(\t)});
        \draw[thick, blue!70!black, dashed, samples=60, domain=0:180, variable=\t]
             plot ({(\TR+\Tr)*cos(\t)}, {\Ts*(\TR+\Tr)*sin(\t)});
        % red: q_1 = 0 = 2pi  (meridian at the seam)
        \draw[very thick, red!80!black, samples=60, domain=-49:131, variable=\t]
             plot ({(\TR+\Tr*cos(\t))*cos(\ug)},
                   {\Ts*(\TR+\Tr*cos(\t))*sin(\ug)+\Tc*\Tr*sin(\t)});
        \draw[thick, red!80!black, dashed, samples=60, domain=131:311, variable=\t]
             plot ({(\TR+\Tr*cos(\t))*cos(\ug)},
                   {\Ts*(\TR+\Tr*cos(\t))*sin(\ug)+\Tc*\Tr*sin(\t)});
        % corner point (0,0) = (2pi,2pi)
        \fill ({(\TR+\Tr)*cos(\ug)}, {\Ts*(\TR+\Tr)*sin(\ug)}) circle (3pt);
        % two points on either side of the seam
        \coordinate (Pa) at ({\TR*cos(\ug+11)}, {\Ts*\TR*sin(\ug+11)+\Tc*\Tr});
        \coordinate (Pb) at ({\TR*cos(\ug-11)}, {\Ts*\TR*sin(\ug-11)+\Tc*\Tr});
        \fill[blue!60!black] (Pa) circle (2.8pt);
        \fill[red!80!black]  (Pb) circle (2.8pt);
        \node[blue!60!black, font=\scriptsize, above right=1pt] at (Pa) {$\varepsilon$};
        \node[red!80!black,  font=\scriptsize, below left=1pt]  at (Pb) {$2\pi{-}\varepsilon$};
        \node[note] at (0,-2.9) {(c) torus $\mathcal{T}^2$};
      \end{scope}
    \end{tikzpicture}}
    \caption{Gluing the opposite sides of the square: identifying
    $q_1=0\equiv 2\pi$ (red) gives a cylinder, identifying
    $q_2=0\equiv 2\pi$ (blue) closes it into a torus
    $\mathcal{T}^2=\mathcal{S}^1\times\mathcal{S}^1$. The points
    $q_1=\varepsilon$ and $q_1=2\pi-\varepsilon$, far apart in the square,
    are now neighbours on either side of the seam: the torus respects the
    topology of $C$. Configuration spaces are in general manifolds
    (angular coordinates).}
    \label{fig:cspace-torus-3d}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Why Topology Matters}
\label{subsec:cspace-why-topology}

The toroidal topology of \( C \) is not a mathematical
curiosity: it has practical consequences.

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Paths and interpolation.} A straight line in the square
          \( [0, 2\pi)^2 \) between \( q_\varepsilon \) and
          \( q_{2\pi-\varepsilon} \) sweeps almost the whole range of the
          first joint, whereas on the torus the shortest path is the short
          arc across the seam, of length \( 2\varepsilon \). Planners must
          wrap coordinates modulo \( 2\pi \), not clamp them.
    \item \textbf{Distance metrics.} The Euclidean distance
          \( \|q - q'\| \) is not the natural metric on \( \mathcal{S}^1 \):
          the correct angular distance is
          \( \min(|q - q'|,\ 2\pi - |q - q'|) \).
    \item \textbf{Continuous parameterizations.} There is no globally
          continuous, single-valued parameterization of \( \mathcal{S}^1 \)
          by a real number: no interval of \( \mathbb{R} \) is homeomorphic
          to the circle, so every real representation \( q \in \mathbb{R} \)
          has a discontinuity at the wrap-around point. Controllers must
          account for this.
\end{itemize}

These issues generalize to any non-Euclidean configuration space:
\( \mathcal{T}^n \) for manipulators with revolute joints, \( SE(3) \) for
free-flying bodies, \( \mathbb{R}^2 \times \mathcal{S}^1 \) for wheeled
mobile robots with orientation.

\begin{nb}[Torus vs.\ Sphere]
The torus \( \mathcal{T}^2 \) and the sphere \( \mathcal{S}^2 \) are both
2-dimensional compact manifolds, but they are \emph{not} homeomorphic: the
torus has genus 1 (one hole), the sphere has genus 0. On the torus, a loop
that wraps around the hole cannot be continuously contracted to a point; on
the sphere, every loop can. This distinction is what makes the topology of
\( C \) matter for planning.
\end{nb}

\section{Constraints}
\label{sec:constraints}

Not every configuration in \( C \) is reachable, and not every motion
between two configurations is admissible: robots are subject to
\emph{constraints}. Two classes matter for this course.

\begin{definition}[Holonomic Constraint]
A constraint is \emph{holonomic} if it can be written as an algebraic
relation among the configuration variables only:
\[
g(q) = 0.
\]
Holonomic constraints reduce the dimension of the accessible
configuration space: the robot is confined to a submanifold of \( C \).
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Nonholonomic Constraint]
A constraint is \emph{nonholonomic} if it involves the velocities and
cannot be integrated into an algebraic relation among the configuration
variables:
\[
a(q, \dot q) = 0,
\qquad \text{not equivalent to } g(q) = 0.
\]
Nonholonomic constraints do \emph{not} reduce the dimension of \( C \),
but they restrict the set of admissible velocities at each
configuration. The robot can still reach any configuration, but not
along arbitrary paths.
\end{definition}

The typical example of a nonholonomic system is a car-like robot or a
differential-drive robot: the no-sideways-slip condition on the wheels
is a nonholonomic constraint. This is the fundamental reason why
planning and control for wheeled mobile robots are harder than for
fixed-base manipulators: the configuration space looks Euclidean, but
the reachable motions are constrained.

\section{C-Obstacles and Free Space}
\label{sec:c-obstacles}

Once \( C \) is defined, obstacles from the workspace must be mapped
into it.

\begin{definition}[C-Obstacle]
Given an obstacle \( O_i \subset W \), the
\emph{C-obstacle} \( CO_i \) is the set of configurations in which
the robot collides with \( O_i \):
\[
CO_i = \{ q \in C : B(q) \cap O_i \neq \emptyset \},
\]
where \( B(q) \) is the volume occupied by the robot at configuration
\( q \).
\end{definition}

The union of all C-obstacles is the \emph{C-obstacle region}:
\[
CO = \bigcup_i CO_i.
\]

\begin{definition}[Free Configuration Space]
The \emph{free configuration space} is the complement of \( CO \) in
\( C \):
\[
C_{\text{free}} = C \setminus CO
= \{ q \in C : B(q) \cap \textstyle\bigcup_i O_i = \emptyset \}.
\]
\end{definition}

Planning a safe motion is equivalent to finding a path in
\( C_{\text{free}} \) connecting the start configuration \( q_s \) to
the goal configuration \( q_g \).

\paragraph{The point-robot trick.}
If the robot is modeled as a point, the workspace obstacles must be
inflated by the Minkowski sum with the robot's geometry. After this
transformation, planning for the extended robot reduces to planning
for a point in \( C_{\text{free}} \). This is the canonical formulation
of the motion planning problem.

\paragraph{Path vs.\ trajectory.}
A \emph{path} is a purely geometric sequence of configurations, with
no timing information. A \emph{trajectory} is a path endowed with a
time law \( q(t) \). In this chapter, and in motion planning in
general, the object of interest is the path. Timing becomes relevant
only when the dynamics and the control layer are brought into play.

\section{Relevance to Motion Planning}
\label{sec:cspace-motion-planning}

The configuration space reformulates the motion planning problem in
a clean and general way:

\begin{quote}
Given \( C \), a start configuration \( q_s \in C_{\text{free}} \),
and a goal configuration \( q_g \in C_{\text{free}} \), find a
continuous path \( \tau : [0, 1] \to C_{\text{free}} \) such that
\( \tau(0) = q_s \) and \( \tau(1) = q_g \).
\end{quote}

All the algorithms studied later in the course --- roadmaps, cell
decompositions, probabilistic planners, potential fields --- are
methods for solving this problem in \( C_{\text{free}} \). The
differences between them lie in how they represent \( C_{\text{free}} \)
and how they search it.

A subtle point: the \emph{topology} of \( C \) matters. Searching for
a path in \( \mathbb{R}^n \) is different from searching in
\( \mathcal{S}^1 \), \( \mathcal{T}^n \), or \( SE(3) \). Planners
must respect the topology of the space in which they operate.

